---
import '../styles/legal.css';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteChrome from '../components/SiteChrome.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

/*
 * The GDPR notice for Munchview, in English, and deliberately only for
 * Munchview.
 *
 * It replaces the Turkish KVKK notice that stood here until 2026-08-16, at
 * the owner's instruction: the site is to be in English throughout. Worth
 * writing down plainly, because the next person will wonder — KVKK and the
 * GDPR are different laws, the studio is established in Turkey, and this page
 * does not discharge a Turkish obligation. /kvkk redirects here so nothing
 * that linked to it breaks.
 *
 * Article 13 prescribes what has to be in here: the controller's identity,
 * the purposes and the lawful basis for each, recipients and any transfer
 * abroad, how long it is kept, and the Chapter III rights with the right to
 * complain to a supervisory authority. Every fact below was checked against
 * the source before it was written — the sync Worker
 * (web/content-api/src/index.js), the snapshot shape (src/sync/snapshot.ts)
 * and the account store (src/store/useAccountStore.ts) in the munchview repo
 * — because a notice that overstates what we hold is as bad as one that hides
 * it.
 *
 * Per-app on purpose: DeftDay's apps differ. Munchview carries no advertising
 * and no analytics; that is a fact about Munchview and must never be copied
 * into another app's notice without checking that app's own code.
 */
const updated = 'August 16, 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Munchview — GDPR Notice — DeftDay Studio"
  description="Who processes your data in Munchview, what is processed and on what lawful basis, how long it is kept, and the rights you have over it."
>
  <SiteChrome />
  <div class="sky" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="s1"></span><span class="s2"></span><span class="s3"></span>
  </div>
  <div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <main id="main" class="legal">
    <span class="eyebrow">Munchview</span>
    <h1>What we process,<br /><span class="serif">and what we do not.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Articles 13 and 14 of the GDPR require whoever processes your personal
      data to tell you who they are, what they process and why, on what lawful
      basis, and what rights you have. This page does that — for the Munchview
      app.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="scope">
      <h2 id="scope">What this covers</h2>
      <p>
        The Munchview app only, and the personal data processed for it.
        DeftDay Studio's apps differ from one another: a sentence that is true
        of one may not be true of the next, so each gets its own notice and no
        single page stands in for all of them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="controller">
      <h2 id="controller">1. Who the controller is</h2>
      <p>
        <b>Controller:</b> DeftDay Studio, Izmir, Turkey.<br />
        <b>Legal enquiries:</b>
        <a href="mailto:legal@deftday.com">legal@deftday.com</a><br />
        <b>Data and deletion requests:</b>
        <a href="mailto:privacy@deftday.com">privacy@deftday.com</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="what">
      <h2 id="what">2. What is processed</h2>
      <p>
        An account is optional and the app works fully without one. The
        distinction starts there.
      </p>
      <p><b>If you use it without an account</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Nothing that identifies you reaches our servers. Categories, tags,
          watch history, likes and profiles stay in your phone's own storage
          and go when you uninstall.
        </li>
        <li>
          The app asks our content service for a list of videos. That request
          carries only the category names or keywords you picked, the length
          you chose and your source preference. No user id, device id or
          advertising id is attached — the app has none.
        </li>
        <li>
          As with any internet request, the IP address it came from reaches the
          server. It is used for one thing: limiting how many requests one
          address may make in a minute. The counter is held under a key
          containing that address and deletes itself after 120 seconds.
        </li>
        <li>
          The country the request came from is read at our infrastructure
          provider's edge, so the app can show the video categories that exist
          where you are. Nothing finer than the country is read, and it is not
          stored against you.
        </li>
        <li>
          The app requests no Android runtime permissions at all: no location,
          contacts, photos, files or microphone.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p><b>If you sign in with Google</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          We receive your Google account id, name, email address and profile
          picture — who you are, and nothing about your YouTube account,
          videos or subscriptions.
        </li>
        <li>
          The list your phone keeps is then also stored by our sync service
          under that account id, so a second device signed in to the same
          account can pick it up.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="why">
      <h2 id="why">3. Why, and on what lawful basis</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Returning a list of videos</b> — necessary to perform the service
          you asked for. <b>Art. 6(1)(b)</b>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Carrying your preferences between your own devices</b> — the same
          basis, and only once you have chosen to sign in. <b>Art. 6(1)(b)</b>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The per-address request counter, and short-lived server logs</b> —
          our legitimate interest in keeping the service available and not
          being overwhelmed. <b>Art. 6(1)(f)</b>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        There is no profiling and no automated decision-making. We do not
        advertise, and we have no analytics or tracking of any kind, so no
        processing here rests on consent for those purposes — because none of
        them happen.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="who">
      <h2 id="who">4. Who else sees it</h2>
      <p>
        We do not sell or share personal data, and we never use it to
        advertise. Two kinds of third party are unavoidable and both are named
        here rather than hidden behind a general clause:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Our infrastructure provider</b> (Cloudflare) hosts the content and
          sync services and processes requests on our behalf.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The video platforms</b> — YouTube, Dailymotion, Vimeo, the
          Internet Archive and PeerTube. When a video plays, your device talks
          to that platform directly, exactly as a browser would, and that
          traffic is governed by their terms and privacy policies rather than
          ours.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Both may process data outside the EEA. Transfers rely on the European
        Commission's standard contractual clauses or an adequacy decision, as
        applicable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="kept">
      <h2 id="kept">5. How long it is kept</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>The synced record</b> — for as long as the account exists. Delete
          the account and it goes.
        </li>
        <li><b>The request counter</b> — 120 seconds.</li>
        <li>
          <b>Server logs</b> — a few days, for reliability and abuse
          prevention, held by our infrastructure provider.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Everything on your phone</b> — until you delete it or uninstall
          the app. It is not ours to keep.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="rights">
      <h2 id="rights">6. Your rights</h2>
      <p>Under Chapter III of the GDPR you have the right to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>know whether we process your data, and obtain a copy — <b>Art. 15</b></li>
        <li>have it corrected — <b>Art. 16</b></li>
        <li>have it erased — <b>Art. 17</b></li>
        <li>restrict how it is processed — <b>Art. 18</b></li>
        <li>receive it in a portable form — <b>Art. 20</b></li>
        <li>object to processing based on legitimate interests — <b>Art. 21</b></li>
        <li>
          complain to a supervisory authority in the country where you live or
          work — <b>Art. 77</b>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write to <a href="mailto:privacy@deftday.com">privacy@deftday.com</a>
        and we will answer within one month. You do not have to write to us to
        delete your data: the app does it directly, in
        <b>You → Your account → Delete my account data</b>, and there is a web
        route at <a href="/munchview/delete-account">deftday.com/munchview/delete-account</a>
        that works without installing anything.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="children">
      <h2 id="children">7. Children</h2>
      <p>
        Munchview is not directed at children and we do not knowingly process
        the data of anyone under 16. The app requests strict safe-search from
        every platform it plays from.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="site">
      <h2 id="site">8. This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site is separate from the app. It uses cookieless analytics that
        set no identifier and build no profile of you. The app itself carries
        no analytics whatsoever.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="legal-block" aria-labelledby="changes">
      <h2 id="changes">9. Changes</h2>
      <p>
        When this notice changes, the date at the top changes with it. The
        fuller English description of the same processing is at
        <a href="/munchview/privacy">the Munchview privacy policy</a>, and
        every DeftDay app is covered on the
        <a href="/privacy">studio privacy page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
